\section{What the page does}{

Prepare the photo as \code{preprocess} says, run the image encoder (for example
with onnxruntime-web), divide \code{image_embeds} by its length to get \code{v}, and
take the dot product of \code{v} with each row of the text embeddings. The
probability of each label is the softmax of \code{logit_scale} times those dot
products, which is what \code{\link[=suggestSpecies]{suggestSpecies()}} computes.
}

\section{Size}{

The image encoder is the download that matters. Use an \code{"int8"} encoder
for the web: 96 MB for CLIP ViT-B/32 and 90 MB for BioCLIP as
\code{\link[=exportOnnxModel]{exportOnnxModel()}} writes them, against about 350 MB unquantised, with
the same answers on dimoose's example photos (BioCLIP 2, a larger model,
is 310 MB even so). Where that is still too much, \code{"mobileclip-s0"} at
\code{"fp16"} is 23 MB, but it is a general model that does not know species
names as BioCLIP does. The text embeddings are tiny
(2 KB per label), and are best made with the \code{"fp32"} text encoder:
\code{onnxModel(name, precision = "int8", textPrecision = "fp32")}.
}

\section{Tested with onnxruntime-web}{

The \code{"int8"}, \code{"fp16"} and \code{"fp32"} encoders that \code{\link[=exportOnnxModel]{exportOnnxModel()}}
writes for CLIP ViT-B/32 and BioCLIP were run in onnxruntime-web 1.31.1 on
its WebAssembly backend (under Node.js) from folders this function wrote,
with the 14 Montana example photos prepared as \code{preprocess} says. All
three load and run there. Against the same file run in Python the lowest
cosine of the embeddings was 0.9996 for \code{"int8"} and 1.0000 for \code{"fp16"}
and \code{"fp32"}, and the most likely label was the same for every photo. The
\code{"int8"} files hold 8-bit weights that are turned back into floats when
the model loads, so they use no integer arithmetic a runtime might lack.
The WebGPU backend was not tested.

Ready-made \verb{_quantized} files from the Hub (\code{encoder = "hub"} with an
\code{"int8"} model) are quantised differently, with integer arithmetic. That
of \code{Xenova/clip-vit-base-patch32} had a lowest cosine of 0.85 with the
\code{"fp32"} embeddings on the same backend and gave another most likely label
for 5 of the 14 photos; its \code{"fp16"} file had 1.0000. Point a page at the
\code{"fp16"} file, or copy in an \code{"int8"} encoder from \code{\link[=exportOnnxModel]{exportOnnxModel()}}.

How the photo is resized matters more than the encoder's precision.
\code{preprocess$interpolation} names the filter the model was trained with
(bicubic, with smoothing when a photo is made smaller, as the Python
library Pillow does it). With BioCLIP on the Montana photos, resizing with
a bilinear filter instead gave embeddings with a cosine of 0.93 to 0.98
(0.97 on average) with the right ones, and nearest-neighbour 0.84 to 0.95.
}

